    {
      "id": "S2.T07.iptables.session-check.dependency",
      "template": "T07",
      "name": "iptables, callers learn that a shared dependency is black-holed: in band on HTTP/2 with a session check",
      "mode": "iptables",
      "trigger": "blackhole",
      "group": "dependency",
      "prediction": {
        "kind": "removal",
        "band": {
          "low": 12,
          "high": 26.105
        },
        "text": "The algebra predicts that the Service has no ready endpoint, and that the rules of each node have no backend. The band is 12 s to 26.105 s after the fault. Redis is black-holed for each replica for the hold (27 s). Each request needs Redis, thus each request fails from the fault until the callers recover. Before the readiness latch of a replica trips (8 s–20 s), each request on it waits for Redis and fails slowly. Then its controller is not ready, and the service refuses each request at once with a 503. The kubelet and the propagation (CAL.T07.iptables: median 0.059 s, 33 times) remove both replicas 12 s–26.105 s after the fault. Then the Service is empty, and its rules refuse each new connection. The lame duck closes each connection after the trip with a GOAWAY on its session, thus each next request opens a new connection, and the empty Service refuses it. After the heal, the client of Redis connects again in 3.1 s at the most, and the readiness of the service recovers up to 6 s later. The kubelet then finds a replica ready in 2 s at the most, and the propagation adds it to the rules. Each client sends a request each 20 ms, thus about 3954–6039 requests fail. A stream timeout before the trip closes its session, and the next stream opens a new one. Thus D is the same as C."
      },
      "decision": "holds",
